<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Borjigin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Borjigin were the Mongol clan to which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> belonged. His ancestors, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabul-khan">Khabul Khan</a>, led the Khamag Mongol confederation in the 12th century, and after the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> descent from Genghis Khan's branch of the clan became the condition for holding the title of khan across the steppe. The Yuan emperors and many later Chinggisid khans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> were Borjigin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/borjigin.jpg" alt="Borjigin" width="320" height="331" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown author Vector: AmygdalicGloss · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imperial_Seal_of_the_Mongols_1246.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Society was hierarchical, with the <b>Borjigin</b> royal clan and a warrior aristocracy at the top, common herders forming the backbone of the economy, and captives, including many skilled artisans, transferred from conquered lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The confederation was a loose alliance of <b>Borjigin</b>, Tayichiud and other clans, choosing khans at assemblies.</blockquote><a href="/state/khamag-mongol">Khamag Mongol</a></li></ul></section>
